================================================================================
Composition
================================================================================

WhisperLoader combines two prior MalDev Academy projects into a single
3-process loader with an added RWX region-reuse optimization:

  - WhisperHijack (Apache License, Version 2.0)
    Copyright 2026 MalDev Academy
    https://github.com/Maldev-Academy/WhisperHijack

    Provides the Thread Name-Calling (TNC) payload delivery primitive
    and the granularity-based target allocation path.

  - TpDirectCallbackHijack (Apache License, Version 2.0)
    Copyright 2026 MalDev Academy
    https://github.com/Maldev-Academy/TpDirectCallbackHijack

    Provides the TP_DIRECT.Callback hijack execution primitive, the
    pool-object walker, the TP_IO / TP_ALPC / TP_JOB internal structure
    layouts, and the per-run restore stub.

Files carried over from these projects retain their original Apache 2.0
headers alongside the WhisperLoader notice.

================================================================================
Techniques - Original Research
================================================================================

The techniques combined in this project were originally described by:

  - hasherezade (Check Point Research)
    Thread Name-Calling (July 2024)
    https://research.checkpoint.com/2024/thread-name-calling-using-thread-name-for-offense/

  - Alon Leviev (SafeBreach Labs)
    "The Pool Party You Will Never Forget: New Process Injection Techniques
    Using Windows Thread Pools" (Black Hat EU 2023)
    https://github.com/SafeBreach-Labs/PoolParty
    https://www.safebreach.com/blog/process-injection-using-windows-thread-pools/

    The TP_DIRECT.Callback hijack primitive, the TP_IO / TP_ALPC / TP_JOB
    object kinds, and the NtSetIoCompletion dispatch trigger all originate
    from the Pool Party research. The specific primitive implemented here
    (walking a victim's live TP_POOL.PoolObjectList and hijacking an
    existing pool object's embedded TP_DIRECT.Callback in place, rather
    than inserting a new TP_<kind> structure, with a per-run restore stub)
    is not one of the eight originally published variants.

All credit for the original research belongs to hasherezade / Check Point
Research and to Alon Leviev / SafeBreach Labs.

Thread pool internal structure layouts (TP_POOL, TPP_CLEANUP_GROUP_MEMBER,
TP_IO, TP_ALPC, TP_JOB, TP_DIRECT) were reconstructed from WinDbg "dt"
output against ntdll shipped with the Windows 10 and Windows 11 builds
listed in InternalStructures.h.

